    "answer": {
      "short": "$(x,y)=(1,2)$ or $(2,1)$.\n\nCombine the logarithms to obtain $xy=2$, then use $x+y=3$.",
      "worked": "The original logarithms require $x>0$ and $y>0$. Using the addition property:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_2(xy)&=1\\\\xy&=2\\end{align*}$$\nFrom $x+y=3$, substitute $y=3-x$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x(3-x)&=2\\\\3x-x^2&=2\\\\x^2-3x+2&=0\\\\(x-1)(x-2)&=0\\\\x&=1\\text{ or }2\\end{align*}$$\nIf $x=1$, then $y=2$. If $x=2$, then $y=1$. Both pairs have positive entries, sum $3$ and product $2$, so they satisfy the original equations.\n\nHence $(x,y)=(1,2)$ or $(2,1)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
